        "math_academy_id": "q-123474",
        "problem": "If ${F}^{\u2032}(t)=\u27e8\\frac{16\\sqrt[3]{t}}{3},\\,5{e}^{5t}\u27e9$ and $F(0)=\u27e82,\\,-1\u27e9,$ then $F(t)=$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{\u2032}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \u27e8\\frac{16\\sqrt[3]{t}}{3},\\,5{e}^{5t}\u27e9\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \u27e8\\frac{16\\sqrt[3]{t}}{3},\\,5{e}^{5t}\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t & =\u27e8\\int \\frac{16\\sqrt[3]{t}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 5{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e84{t}^{4/3}+{c}_{1},\\,{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition $F(0)=\u27e82,\\,-1\u27e9$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0)=\u27e84\\cdot {0}^{4/3}+{c}_{1},\\,{e}^{5\\cdot 0}+{c}_{2}\u27e9 & =\u27e82,\\,-1\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e8{c}_{1},\\,1+{c}_{2}\u27e9 & =\u27e82,\\,-1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=-2.$ Consequently\n\n$$\nF(t)=\u27e84{t}^{4/3}+2,\\,{e}^{5t}-2\u27e9.\n$$",
